## Screenshot CSS — Honest Technical Assessment

You asked about this CSS:
```css
.protected-content {
  -webkit-user-select: none;
  user-select: none;
}
* {
  -webkit-touch-callout: none;
}
```

### What it actually does ✅

| Property | What it does |
|---|---|
| `user-select: none` | Prevents text selection and Ctrl+C copy of text — **genuinely useful** |
| `-webkit-touch-callout: none` | Prevents iOS "tap & hold" menu (Save Image, Copy) — **genuinely useful on iPhone** |

### What the comment gets wrong ❌

> "This blacks out content when screenshots are taken on some OS"

**This is a myth.** Neither property blacks out content during screenshots on any mainstream OS:
- **Windows** (Print Screen, Win+Shift+S) — not blocked, content visible
- **macOS** (Cmd+Shift+3/4) — not blocked, content visible  
- **Android** (Power+Volume) — not blocked, content visible
- **iOS** (Side button+Volume) — not blocked, content visible

The only way to black out a web view from OS screenshots is `FLAG_SECURE` in a **native Android/iOS app** — it doesn't exist in a browser.

### What we'll actually implement ✅

We will include your CSS properties **plus** the full Level 3 stack:

```
1. user-select: none          → blocks text copy  
2. -webkit-touch-callout: none → blocks iOS tap-hold  
3. Keyboard blocker           → Ctrl+S, Ctrl+P, Ctrl+A, Ctrl+C on the viewer  
4. Right-click disabled       → on the canvas element  
5. @media print { display:none } → blocks browser print-to-PDF  
6. Canvas rendering (PDF.js)  → PDF is rendered as pixels, not as a selectable/saveable file  
7. Dynamic watermark          → user name + masked email burned on every page  
8. 5-minute signed URL        → even if intercepted, URL expires quickly  
```

> [!NOTE]
> The **watermark is the real security backbone**. A student who shares a screenshot exposes themselves. This deterrent is why platforms like Unacademy use it — not because they can prevent OS screenshots, but because they can *identify who leaked it*.

